  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY NIGHT THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 320 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nQuiet and dry weather continues at least through midweek as the \nupper level ridge builds over the Mississippi Valley and the \nsurface high drifts across the Ohio Valley and into the Mid Atlantic \nRegion.  Highs warm up to near seasonal normals in the mid 50s by \nMonday, and then further into the upper 50s Tuesday, and then 60s on \nWednesday and Thursday as southerly flow increases ahead of the next \nupstream short wave. Global guidance shows little moisture return \nthrough at least Tuesday with ridging over the eastern 1/2 of the \nCONUS blocking the Gulf, so the sky should remain clear to mostly \nclear through that period. The clear sky and continuing light winds \novernight will allow temperatures to fall into the 20s again Monday \nand Tuesday mornings in most locations.  However, increasing \nsoutherly flow on the western side of the surface ridge will keep \ntemperatures from falling as much on Wednesday morning except in \nsheltered locations.  Then there should be some moisture return into \nthe region Wednesday and Wednesday night as a short wave crosses the \nRockies and forces cyclogenesis over the Plains. Deterministic \nguidance is in pretty good agreement up to that point in the \nforecast, but begins diverging Thursday into Friday.  There's \ndisagreement between the GFS and ECMWF on the amplitude and \norientation of the short wave as it moves from the Plains into the \nMidwest Wednesday night into Thursday.  This leads to differences in \nthe strength of the surface low, how far southeast the associated \ncold front travels, and how much moisture is pulled up into the Mid \nMississippi Valley ahead of it.  The GFS is largely dry for Thursday \nand Friday, but the ECMWF is showing stronger moisture return and \nprints out around 1/2 an inch of QPF. About 20-30 percent of the \nLREF members have precip for our area which agrees well with the \nlatest NBM forecast.  Therefore chance PoPs for the end of the \nforecast period continues to look reasonable, along with \ntemperatures warming well above normal in southwest flow ahead \nof the front.",
